        <h2>Why People Switch from Snus to Pouches</h2>
        <p>
          The shift from snus to nicotine pouches has accelerated across Scandinavia over the past five years, and it is not hard to see why. <strong>No staining</strong> is the number-one reason people cite. Brown snus pouches discolour teeth over time &mdash; white nicotine pouches simply do not. If you care about your smile, this alone is enough reason to switch.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Discretion</strong> matters too. White pouches are virtually invisible under the lip, with no drip and no spit. You can use them in meetings, on flights, or during a date without anyone noticing. The flavour range is also dramatically wider. Where snus offers perhaps a dozen traditional profiles, brands like <a href="/brands/loop">LOOP</a> and <a href="/brands/velo">VELO</a> release new flavours quarterly &mdash; from watermelon to espresso.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then there is the practical side. <strong>Nicotine pouches don't need refrigeration</strong>, which makes them easier to store and ship. They are legal everywhere in the EU, so you can order them online without worrying about customs. Brands with roots in the snus world &mdash; like <a href="/brands/skruf">Skruf</a> and <a href="/brands/lundgrens">Lundgrens</a> &mdash; now produce nicotine pouches alongside their traditional lines, which tells you where the industry sees the future heading.
        </p>

        <h2>Strength: How Do They Compare?</h2>
        <p>
          Both snus and nicotine pouches can be very strong. The key complication is that <strong>snus strength is often measured in mg/g</strong> (milligrams per gram of filling), while nicotine pouches typically use <strong>mg/pouch</strong>. This makes direct comparison tricky. A snus labelled "14mg/g" in a 1-gram pouch delivers roughly 14mg per pouch &mdash; but pouch weights vary, so the actual dose depends on format.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the top end, the strongest snus products reach around 45mg per pouch. Nicotine pouches go even further &mdash; some extreme products hit 50mg. For a full breakdown of high-strength options, see our <a href="/blog/strongest-nicotine-pouches-ranked-2026">strongest nicotine pouches ranking</a>. If you are new to either product, start low. Read our <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-your-strength">strength guide</a> before experimenting at the top end.
        </p>

        <h2>Which Should You Choose?</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Choose snus if</strong> you value traditional tobacco flavour and the heritage of a 200-year-old Swedish product &mdash; and if you live in Sweden or can legally source it where you are. Snus delivers a distinct, earthy character that nicotine pouches do not replicate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Choose nicotine pouches if</strong> you want no tobacco, no staining, no spitting, and maximum flavour variety. If you live anywhere in the EU outside Sweden, nicotine pouches are your only legal option for an oral nicotine product. They are also the better choice if you are <a href="/blog/switching-from-cigarettes-to-nicotine-pouches">switching from cigarettes</a> and want to avoid tobacco entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          For most people reading this, nicotine pouches are the practical answer. Browse our full <a href="/nicotine-pouches">nicotine pouch range</a> or start with our <a href="/blog/best-nicotine-pouches-for-beginners-2026">beginner's guide</a> if you are not sure where to start.
        </p>

      </div>

        <h2>The Legality Difference: Why Pouches Are Legal Everywhere in the EU (and Snus Isn't)</h2>
        <p>
          Nicotine pouches and snus look similar and deliver nicotine in nearly identical ways, but one critical difference shapes availability across Europe: legality. This difference isn't a technicality — it affects where you can buy, how products are marketed, and what regulations apply.
        </p>
        <h3>The EU Snus Ban</h3>
        <p>
          The European Union banned the sale of snus (moist tobacco pouches) in 2004 under the Tobacco Products Directive, with one exception: Sweden. When Sweden joined the EU in 1995, it negotiated an exemption allowing continued snus sales as part of a public health compromise — Sweden had established snus use as a harm-reduction pathway for smokers, and the exemption recognised this existing market. No other EU country was granted the same allowance. Today, importing snus into Germany, Denmark, France, the UK, or any non-Swedish EU state is technically illegal for retail sale, though enforcement varies (online black markets exist).
        </p>
        <h3>Why Pouches Escaped the Ban</h3>
        <p>
          Nicotine pouches emerged after 2010 as a tobacco-free product. Because they contain no actual tobacco — only nicotine extract, fibres, and flavourings — they fall outside the original snus ban. Regulatory bodies, including the European Commission, determined that tobacco-free nicotine pouches represent a different product category that could be legal under different rules. This created a regulatory opening: pouches are now sold legally across the entire EU, including in Germany, France, and every country where snus is prohibited.
        </p>
        <h3>What This Means Practically</h3>
        <p>
          You can walk into a convenience store or order online from any EU retailer and legally purchase nicotine pouches. Snus requires either a trip to Sweden or purchasing through grey-market online channels in other countries. For businesses, pouches are the legally safe product to stock and promote; snus creates compliance and liability risks outside Sweden. This is why retailers (particularly in countries like Germany and France) aggressively promote pouches while snus remains underground.
        </p>
        <h3>The Health Rationale</h3>
        <p>
          Regulators banned snus partly due to concerns about oral cancer and gum disease linked to tobacco's by-products, not nicotine itself. Nicotine pouches, containing no tobacco, don't carry the same carcinogenic risk profile. The UK's NHS and Swedish public health authorities both treat pouches as a legitimate harm-reduction tool compared to smoking. The EU's ban on snus was about tobacco risk, not nicotine; pouches sidestep this entirely by eliminating tobacco.
        </p>
        <h3>Future Regulatory Trajectory</h3>
        <p>
          As of 2026, the legal landscape strongly favours pouches. Sweden has signalled no plans to rescind its snus exemption, and there's zero appetite within the EU to ban pouches — they're seen as a positive alternative to smoking in most health contexts. If you're a user in Germany, France, Italy, or anywhere outside Sweden, pouches represent your only legal nicotine pouch option without grey-market risk.
        </p>

      </div>

